<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hisham I of Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/757">757</a>–796</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Hisham I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hisham I was the second Umayyad emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, ruling from 788 to 796, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>. A pious ruler who favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law, he sent annual summer campaigns against the Christian north, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> in 794 and 795, and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> in 793. He completed his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Hisham I of Córdoba" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_hisham_i_21829.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
